            <ArticleTitle>[Distribution of the electrocardiographic changes characteristic of ischemic heart disease among the male population of Leningrad in comparison with the clinical biochemical indices].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>71-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors studied the peculiarities of electrocardiograms recorded under conditions of rest in 1,022 males from 40 to 59 years of age, who reflected representatively a selected unorganized population of 1,250 persons. The ECG were analysed according to the Minnesota code. Among the examined persons 9.6% were found to have &quot;coronary&quot; ECG changes indicating to ischemic heart disease. The tread-mill test conducted in individuals chosen for scrupulous examination made it possible to make a diagnosis of ischemic heart disease in 19.6% of 219 persons (72 of whom had hyperlipidemia). A tendency toward relative decrease in high-density lipoproteins was noted in individuals with clearly manifested signs of coronary pathology on the ECG.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
